"A Merkle tree diagram shows how leaf hashes combine into a root and how a compact proof establishes membership under stated hashing rules.\n\nUse when: Explain integrity verification or inclusion proofs. Review which sibling hashes a verifier needs.\n\nAvoid when: The tree represents ordinary domain containment without cryptographic aggregation.\n\nVisual grammar: Leaves identify data blocks or their hashes. Internal nodes show ordered hash combination under the named construction. Highlighted paths and sibling nodes identify a proof to the root.\n\nBuild: Name the hash algorithm and tree construction, including domain separation if used. Choose a small synthetic leaf set. Compute or label schematic parent hashes consistently. Lay out levels evenly and place proof annotations beside, not across, tree edges.\n\nCheck: Child ordering and odd-leaf handling follow the selected construction. The proof includes the correct sibling hashes and positions. A root’s trusted origin is distinguished from mere hash consistency. Hash labels remain readable without crossing proof highlights.\n\nPitfalls: Claiming a valid proof establishes the truth or safety of the underlying data. Omitting ordering rules and creating an ambiguous root calculation.\n\nExample: A verifier checks one log entry using its leaf hash and the sibling hashes on the path to a trusted root.\n\nAlternatives: PKI certificate chain, Software supply-chain provenance graph."
